# Bonus0 - Buffer Overflow (Stack + NOP Sled)

## Context

The program reads two inputs with `read()` then concatenates them with `strcpy` and `strcat` without size check. By overflowing the destination buffer, we overwrite the return address to redirect execution to our shellcode stored in `p()`'s large temporary buffer on the stack.

---

## Program flow

```c
main()
  → pp(dest)          // concatenates two inputs into dest
      → p(local_34)   // reads input 1, strncpy 20 bytes
      → p(local_20)   // reads input 2, strncpy 20 bytes
      → strcpy(dest, local_34)   // no size check !
      → dest[strlen] = ' '       // adds space
      → strcat(dest, local_20)   // no size check !
```

---

## Why p()'s large buffer matters

```asm
sub $0x1018,%esp          ; allocates 4096 bytes temporary buffer
read(0, buffer, 0x1000)   ; reads up to 4096 bytes
strncpy(dest, buffer, 20) ; copies only 20 bytes to dest
```

```
p() temporary buffer = 4096 bytes at 0xbfffe680
strncpy copies only 20 bytes → rest stays in large buffer !
```

The shellcode placed beyond 20 bytes **remains in memory** on the stack after `p()` returns — we can jump there !

---

## Memory layout

### Stack during pp()

```
ebp-0x30 = local_34  [20 bytes]  ← input 1, strncpy destination
ebp-0x1c = local_20  [20 bytes]  ← input 2, strncpy destination
ebp-0x08 = saved edi
ebp-0x04 = saved ebx
ebp+0x00 = saved ebp
ebp+0x04 = return address        ← we overwrite this
```

### dest in main()

```asm
sub $0x40,%esp
lea 0x16(%esp),%eax   ; dest = esp+0x16
```

```
dest        = 0xbffff736  (seen in ltrace)
return addr = 0xbffff73c + 4 = 0xbffff740
distance    = 54 bytes

20 (local_34) + 1 (space) = 21 bytes already written
54 - 21 = 33 bytes from local_20
33 - 20 (local_20) = 13 bytes padding before return address
13 - 4 (address) = 9 bytes before address
```

